WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.040
I Am Legend, the 2007 blockbuster starring
Will Smith and a bunch of CG zombie things

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.769
called Dark Seekers, was pretty popular, becoming
the highest-grossing film of the year.

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:16.430
It raked in over $500 million worldwide, and
Warner Brothers immediately greenlit a sequel

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.590
— but it never happened.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.500
Here's why we never got to see I Am Legend
2.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.140
Will Smith never really seemed into it

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.920
In 2012, Will Smith spoke with the BBC to
promote the upcoming Men in Black III.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.220
When asked about appearing in a follow-up
to I Am Legend, Smith didn't waste his words:

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.480
"Producers are working on it.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.840
I'm not actually working on it.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.000
If it's great, I'm into it.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.800
I don't want to be the sequel guy."

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.400
When you're promoting a sequel, and then say
that you don't want to be "the sequel guy"

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.020
in relation to another project that's getting
a sequel...well, that's pretty much that.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.510
To his credit, Smith's been pretty much true
to his word.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.969
He's acted in over 20 movies, and he's only
appeared in three sequels: Men in Black II

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:55.649
and III, and Bad Boys II, and that movie was
worth it just for this:

00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:57.930
"---- just got real."

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:03.920
And let's not forget he declined to come back
to the Independence Day sequel in 2016 — and

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.710
not even the lovely Margot Robbie could convince
him otherwise.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.510
"Why'd it take so long?"

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:09.590
"I'm not doing it!

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.820
I'm not, don't get that, don't get that look
on-"

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.760
"I love that movie so much."

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.390
"So you're not doing it?"

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.390
"I'm not...ok"

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.450
If he can say no to her, he is definitely
not the sequel guy.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.450
Prequel plans

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.909
Still, even without Smith, doing a follow-up
to I Am Legend might've been tough.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:30.229
As you may recall, Smith's character, Robert
Neville, died at the end of the original film,

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:33.329
which makes it kind of difficult to pick up
the story where it left off.

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:36.240
Then again, that didn't stop Terminator 3.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.860
"I'm back."

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.350
Anyway, that's why Warner Bros. started pursuing
a prequel, which, according to a 2008 article

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.790
in Variety, would've shown "the final days
of humanity in New York before a man-made

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:52.490
virus caused a plague that left Smith's character
the lone survivor among a mutated mob in the

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.490
city."

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.140
That sounds kind of cool, except the first
movie already used flashbacks to show the

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.380
military quarantine of the city.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.280
Neville valiantly decided to stay behind,
and then helplessly watched as his wife and

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.280
daughter die in a helicopter crash during
the evacuation.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.640
That kind of covers the dramatic beats leading
up to his solitary existence in the original

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:11.640
film.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.640
So...nope.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.510
Rather, a reboot

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.420
Roughly four years after the prequel plans
hit a dead end, Warner Bros. announced yet

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.810
another plan to develop the franchise.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.200
According to Deadline in 2012:

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.490
"The film is not being called a prequel, which
had been rumored for the past few years."

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.950
By 2014, and with Will Smith having famously
declared himself "not the sequel guy," the

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.450
project took yet another turn.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:38.110
This time, according to another Deadline article,
as a "a sci-fi version of The Searchers."

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.120
"I hope you die."

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.450
"That'll be the day."

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.670
That's right, The Searchers, the John Wayne
Western from 1956 that follows a posse of

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.069
cowboys on a mission to get revenge and retrieve
a kidnapped girl.

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:55.580
But probably in this case, the kidnapped girl
is, like, a zombie or something?

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.620
One producer said it'll never happen

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.500
I Am Legend scribe and blockbuster-generator
Akiva Goldsman had been the go-to guy for

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.840
the sequel plans from the beginning, but even
he apparently had his doubts about a new installment

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.840
ever happening.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.410
He laid it all out to io9:

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:10.909
"We've tried every which way.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:13.379
In fact if you're available you could be in
the next movie.

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:16.780
It will never happen but we really enjoyed
trying to make it happen."

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:17.920
Zombie fatigue

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.330
Maybe the biggest problem with the I Am Legend
sequel is coming up with an idea that seems,

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.520
well, fresh.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.220
Our research reveals that, since 28 Days Later
reignited the genre in 2002 the number of

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.710
zombie-related projects has climbed to approximately
a gazillion.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:36.879
Sure, I Am Legend was one of the most successful
zombie flicks out of a solid group that includes

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:41.840
Dawn of the Dead, Zombieland, and World War
Z, but that was mostly due to its early position

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.299
in the new zombie wave, as well as Will Smith's
star power—both of which have significantly

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:47.710
faded in recent years.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:52.230
The Walking Dead still draws solid ratings,
but on the big screen, it's a different story.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.720
For proof, see Pride and Prejudice and Zombies.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.730
Or don't—no one else did.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.400
"Aaahhh!!"

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.970
Warner Bros. would be smart just to let this
one go.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:05.140
After all…does anything really good ever
come from resurrecting the dead?

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.260
Thanks for watching!

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:09.659
